# {{projectName}}

A [SvelteKit](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit) website that reads LumiBase content
in **server-side loads** and runs on Cloudflare Pages, with draft preview.

Pages are rendered by a Pages Function (`@sveltejs/adapter-cloudflare`) at
request time, so a publish in Studio shows up without a rebuild. Every CMS call
happens in `src/lib/server/` — never in the browser.

## Quick start

```bash
cp .env.example .env   # LUMIBASE_URL, LUMIBASE_SITE_ID, LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
npm install
npm run dev            # http://localhost:5173
```

With `LUMIBASE_URL` or the key empty, the site renders a "connect your CMS"
page instead of an error.

## What the CMS needs to provide

This site only reads. Whoever runs the LumiBase instance provides:

1. **A publishable key** — Studio → Access → API keys, marked publishable (the
   token starts with `lbk_pub_`). The site refuses any other kind of token in
   `LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`.
2. **A read grant restricted to published rows.** `GET /api/v1/items` applies
   no published-only filter of its own; normal requests ask for
   `status=published` explicitly, but the grant is what actually enforces it.
3. **A `posts` collection** with `title`, `slug` and `body` fields — or change
   the collection in `src/lib/server/lumibase.ts` and regenerate types.

No CMS yet? `npm create lumibase my-cms -- --template nextjs` scaffolds one
that runs locally in Docker (CMS + Studio + Postgres) and creates exactly the
key and grant above. For hosting, see the one-click deploys:
https://docs.lumibase.dev/en/docs/deployment/one-click

## Environment

All variables are declared in `src/env.ts` and read through
`$app/env/private`, a server-only module: SvelteKit fails the build if browser
code imports it. None is `static`, so on Cloudflare Pages they are read from
the project's Variables and Secrets when the app starts, not baked into the
build.

## Typed content

`src/lib/lumibase-types.ts` holds the generated types for your collections; the
version shipped here matches the `posts` collection described above.
Regenerate it from your real schema:

```bash
LUMIBASE_TOKEN=… npm run types        # writes src/lib/lumibase-types.ts
npm run types:check                   # CI: fails when the file is stale
```

`LUMIBASE_TOKEN` needs schema read access, so it is a secret. It is only used
by these two commands, never by the site.

## Draft preview

Preview is **off** until both of these are set (server-side only):

| Variable | Value |
|---|---|
| `LUMIBASE_PREVIEW_SECRET` | A random string of 32+ characters (`openssl rand -base64 32`) |
| `LUMIBASE_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | A secret token (`lbk_…`, not `lbk_pub_…`) whose grant includes drafts |

Then open, or configure as the preview URL in Studio:

```
https://<your-site>/api/preview?secret=<LUMIBASE_PREVIEW_SECRET>&redirect=/posts/<slug>
```

The browser receives an HttpOnly cookie signed with the secret, valid for one
hour. Requests carrying it read with the preview token (drafts included) and
are served `Cache-Control: private, no-store`; everyone else reads with the
publishable key and gets `public, max-age=0, s-maxage=60`, which a shared
cache in front of the site may honour. A wrong secret is a `401`,
and with preview not configured the route is a `404`. `/api/preview/exit`
ends the session; rotating the secret ends every session at once. The redirect
only accepts same-origin paths.

The LumiBase Delivery API's own `?preview=true` flag
(`/api/v1/deliver/page/:site/:slug?preview=true`) only bypasses its caches — it
does not return drafts. That is why preview here uses a separate token rather
than a query flag.

## Deploy to Cloudflare Pages

`npm run build` writes the Pages output (static assets plus `_worker.js`) to
`.svelte-kit/cloudflare`; `wrangler.jsonc` names that directory and the Pages
project (`{{projectName}}`).

**Option A — Git integration.** Connect the repository in the Cloudflare
dashboard with build command `npm run build` and output directory
`.svelte-kit/cloudflare`, then add the variables above — the key, preview
secret and preview token as **secrets**.

**Option B — direct upload** from your machine or CI:

```bash
npm run build
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=… CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=… npm run deploy
npx wrangler pages secret put LUMIBASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
```

In CI, keep `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` / `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` in repository
secrets and guard the deploy step with
`if: env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN != ''` so pull requests from forks still build.

To run the built site locally under the Pages runtime, put the variables in
`.dev.vars` (git-ignored) and run `npm run preview`.

## Fully static instead

Prefer static HTML, like the Astro starter? Install
`@sveltejs/adapter-static`, use it in `vite.config.ts`, add
`export const prerender = true;` to `src/routes/+layout.server.ts`, and delete
`src/routes/api/preview` and `src/hooks.server.ts` — a static build has no
server to read a preview cookie. The site is written to `build/`, so set
`pages_build_output_dir` in `wrangler.jsonc` (and the Pages output directory)
to `build`. Content is then fixed at build time, so
rebuild on publish with a Pages deploy hook triggered from a LumiBase Flow
(see the Astro starter guide).

## Visual editing

LumiBase visual editing (the planned `@lumibase/visual-editing` package) is not
released yet. `src/lib/components/VisualEditing.svelte` is the hook point: the
root layout renders it only for verified preview sessions, and it is empty
today.

## Scripts

| Script | What it does |
|---|---|
| `npm run dev` | Vite dev server |
| `npm run build` | Cloudflare Pages build into `.svelte-kit/cloudflare` |
| `npm run preview` | Build, then serve it with `wrangler pages dev` |
| `npm run typecheck` | `svelte-kit sync` + `svelte-check` |
| `npm run types` / `types:check` | Generate / verify `src/lib/lumibase-types.ts` |
| `npm run deploy` | `wrangler pages deploy` |
